WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.958 --> 00:00:06.409
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:24.620
K. MICHAEL HAYS: When you're walking through the city of Berlin,

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.750
you're almost in it before you know it.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:35.310
The blocks at the edges of the site are very, very low, almost like benches,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.160
almost like park benches.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.000
But very, very quickly, this undulating ground descends,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.940
and the height of the blocks begins to change and come up very quickly.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.540
At first, the project identifies itself with the city.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.200
There's something about the gridded blocks that are kind of echoed

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.960
in some of those surrounding facades.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.700
The abstraction of the blocks also creates a contrast with the greenery,

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.980
with the trees and plants of the Tiergarten.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.530
At first, the project identifies itself with the city.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:15.640
But then, when you get into the project, you begin to lose the horizon.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.640
The last trees of the Tiergarten start to be

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.000
submerged below the horizon or the blocks raise up to cover the horizon.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.304
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:32.260
Very quickly, you lose the sense of a grid, and the sense of a labyrinth

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.220
starts to take over, and you lose orientation.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.405
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.900
Because there is no marked entry, there are

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.940
many places you can enter the project.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.190
There's also no clear exit.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.320
And very, very quickly, you feel like you're

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.508
in an enclosed world with no exit.

00:01:51.508 --> 00:01:54.994
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.560
In May 2005, the Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europe

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.250
opened in Berlin Mitte, which is the centermost borough

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.760
of the city of Berlin.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.050
The project was designed by the American architect Peter Eisenman.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.470
In some ways, it's a fairly simple project to describe.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:20.870
It comprises almost 3,000 stelae, which are these concrete pillars

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.820
or blocks, arrayed across a site of undulating ground,

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.930
on a site that's almost the size of three football fields.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:36.710
In other ways, it's a very difficult, both conceptually and materially,

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.260
a very difficult project to describe.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:44.090
It took almost 17 years to complete, not only because of construction issues,

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:49.930
but because of debates surrounding the project, which we'll talk about later.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.810
The memorial is dedicated to the Jewish victims

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.090
of Nazi genocide in World War II.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.460
We haven't seen a project quite like this in this course.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.150
For one thing, it's the first 21st-century project that we've seen.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.330
But second, the project really doesn't have a program,

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.400
like a factory or a church.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.920
It doesn't have to provide or house the function of worship,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.730
as it would a church, or the function of work, as it would in a factory.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.310
The project is an architectural memorial.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.210
But what it memorializes is not the life of a single person,

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:30.080
like some great leader or humanitarian, and it's not really

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.260
even the lives of a people, notwithstanding

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.560
that it is a memorial to the murdered Jews of Europe.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.260
What the program actually calls for is to induce memory of a structural

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:47.090
condition of society, of an event in history that should not be forgotten,

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:52.850
that cannot be forgotten, because of the horror of the event itself.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.100
I'll try to show how the project constructs

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.410
what I'll be calling critical memory--

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:03.370
a kind of memory that is reflective, is contemplative.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:08.300
And critical memory will be one of the themes of this presentation.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.380
The second theme is the abstraction of the architecture

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.880
as a response to the need for critical memory, or abstraction as a way

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.399
of producing critical memory.

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:23.660
Scholars who have documented and chronicled the two-stage competition

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:27.230
process by which Peter Eisenman received the commission

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:31.880
have noted, again and again, that Eisenman was the most abstract

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.670
of the 19 entries to the competition.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:39.950
And we'll want to ask why abstraction was the sort of mode of architecture

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:46.450
that was chosen to produce this work or invite this work of critical memory.

